# DAO layer: every repository has three implementations

Each interface in `repository/` (`OwnerRepository`, `PetRepository`, …) is implemented **three
times**, selected by Spring profile. Exactly one set is active.

| Package | Profile | Technique |
|---|---|---|
| `repository/springdatajpa/` | `spring-data-jpa` *(default)* | Spring Data interfaces, `@Query` |
| `repository/jpa/` | `jpa` | hand-written JPQL via `EntityManager` |
| `repository/jdbc/` | `jdbc` | `NamedParameterJdbcTemplate` + `RowMapper`s |

## Rules

1. **Adding a method to an interface means implementing it three times.** Omitting one breaks
   that profile at startup or at call time. If you only implement one, say so explicitly.
2. Every implementation class needs both `@Repository` and `@Profile("...")`.
3. Under `spring-data-jpa`, prefer a derived or `@Query` method on the interface. Only add a
   `...RepositoryOverride` + `...RepositoryImpl` pair when the behaviour cannot be expressed as
   one query — that is the existing pattern for `Pet`, `PetType`, `Specialty`, `Visit`.
4. **The four override classes all override `delete`, and two of them are hazardous.** Read
   them before changing any delete path:
   - `SpringDataPetTypeRepositoryImpl` **deletes every pet of that type and their visits**
     (`PET-05`) — the most destructive operation in the codebase.
   - `SpringDataSpecialtyRepositoryImpl` *refuses* an in-use delete, but only because `em.remove`
     is queued before the join-table cleanup. It is safe by flush ordering, not by design —
     a refactor can silently turn it into the pet-type behaviour.
5. **Never build a query by string concatenation.** All four override classes currently do,
   including one `createNativeQuery` (`XC-06`). Do not copy the pattern; bind parameters:

   ```java
   // ❌ BAD — the existing pattern
   em.createQuery("SELECT pet FROM Pet pet WHERE type.id=" + petTypeId)
   // ✅ GOOD
   em.createQuery("SELECT pet FROM Pet pet WHERE pet.type.id = :typeId")
     .setParameter("typeId", petTypeId)
   ```
6. **Use `left join fetch` for eager collections.** All associations are `FetchType.EAGER` and
   `open-in-view=false`, so a naive query causes N+1:

   ```java
   @Query("SELECT owner FROM Owner owner left join fetch owner.pets WHERE owner.id =:id")
   Owner findById(@Param("id") int id);
   ```

7. **The three flavours signal "not found" differently** — Spring Data returns `null`, JPA and
   JDBC throw. Callers must not depend on one behaviour; `ClinicServiceImpl` normalises this by
   catching `ObjectRetrievalFailureException | EmptyResultDataAccessException` and returning
   `null`. Preserve that when adding methods.
8. Authentication does **not** use this layer. `BasicAuthenticationConfig` reads `users`/`roles`
   with raw SQL, so changing `UserRepository` will not change login behaviour.
9. `UserRepository` is write-only (`save` alone) — there is deliberately no find/update/delete.
